import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
---
<Base title="Who They Married"
  eyebrow="Four families the Žubrinići and Blaževići kept marrying"
  dek="A pedigree takes one person per generation and drops the rest. Look instead at who a family marries repeatedly and different things appear: a German name inside the Military Frontier, a Krivi Put family that married the Blaževići nine times, six marriages into one Otočac household, and an adoption working its way through three generations of surnames.">

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Correction, 13 September 2026</div>
    <h2>Šojat is a family, not a by-name</h2>
    <p class="lede">This archive recorded <span class="mono">Sojat</span> as <q>the by-name the
    tree attaches to one Blažević household</q> — reading it as a <em>nadimak</em> like
    <a href={u("/name")}><em>Papa</em> on the Antići</a>. <strong>That was wrong.</strong></p>
    <p>The Šojati are a <strong>Krivi Put family with fifteen people, dates and a place of their
    own</strong>. <strong>Marko Šojat, born 19 April 1801 at Krivi Put, died 4 February 1871</strong>,
    with five children whose births and deaths are all recorded — Šimun 1819, Marija 1825, Matija
    1828, Mate 1830, Martin 1831. One of them is entered at a place called
    <strong><span class="mono">Sojat Dol 54</span></strong> — a valley named after the family.</p>
    <p style="margin-top:1rem"><strong>And they married the Blaževići nine times.</strong></p>
    <table class="reg">
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Marija Šojat <span class="mono dim">1825–1896</span></td><td>m. Anton “Kljapo” Blažević · <strong>9 children</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Kate Sojat <span class="mono dim">b. 1844</span></td><td>m. Joso Blažević</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Marija Sojat</td><td>m. Antona Blažević · 4 children</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Mare Sojat</td><td>m. Antone Blažević · 2 children</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Marija Sojat</td><td>m. Mile Blažević</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Nikola Šojat <span class="mono dim">b. 1878</span></td><td>m. Kata Blažević</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Maria Sojath <span class="mono dim">1823–1891</span></td><td>m. Domianus Perpić · 4 children</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Ivka Sojat</td><td>m. Dametar Perpić</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Ivan Sojat</td><td>m. Ivka Prpić</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p style="margin-top:1rem">So <span class="mono">Blažević (Sojat)</span> in the tree is almost
    certainly <strong>a household known by the family it married</strong> — which is how a nadimak
    is born, and the opposite of what this archive said. <strong>The mechanism was right and the
    direction was backwards.</strong></p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>Untersinger — a German name inside the Frontier</h2>
    <p class="lede">Seven people, and unusually for this archive they are a <strong>complete
    three-generation household with dates</strong>: <strong>Lucas Untersinger</strong> →
    <strong>George, born 25 March 1787, died 1848</strong>, married to Helena → five children,
    Rosalia 1802, <strong>Ana 1807–1845</strong>, Michael 1810–1840, Lucia 1827, Eva 1839.</p>
    <p><strong>Ana Untersinger married Elias “Ilija” Žubrinić</strong>, and they both died in
    <strong>1845</strong>, leaving seven children — the event
    <a href={u("/open-questions")}>question 4b</a> suspects was an epidemic in Lika.</p>
    <p style="margin-top:1rem"><strong>A German surname in Lika is not an accident.</strong> The
    Military Frontier was administered from Vienna and its officer, forestry and clerical grades
    were staffed from across the monarchy. A Žubrinić marrying an Untersinger in the 1820s is a
    Frontier family marrying into the <em>administration</em> of the Frontier — which fits
    <a href={u("/frontier")}>the six salaried posts</a> this family held.</p>
    <p class="dek"><strong>What would test it:</strong> the Otočac registers for George Untersinger
    (1787) and his father Lucas, and whether either is entered with a rank or an office. If the
    Untersingers arrived as Frontier staff rather than as settlers, the entry will say so.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>Kostelac — six marriages into one household</h2>
    <p class="lede">Fifteen people, and <strong>six of them married Žubrinići</strong>: Stephanus
    married Barbara Žubrinić; Maria Kostelacz, Juliana Kostelacz <em>and</em> Maria Kostelac each
    married a Joannes Žubrinić; Martinus married Francisca Žubrinić; Michaël married Lucia Žubrinić.
    A seventh, Mande Kostelac, married a <strong>Sekula</strong>.</p>
    <p>Two consequences. <strong>Three women of one surname married men of one name</strong> — which
    is exactly the soil in which <a href={u("/open-questions")}>the Michaël Žubrinić confusion</a>
    grew, and a warning about how little a name identifies here.</p>
    <p style="margin-top:1rem"><strong>And one couple is in the tree twice.</strong>
    <span class="mono">Stephanus Kostelacz + Barbara Žubrinić</span> and
    <span class="mono">Stipan Kostelac + Bare Žubrinić</span> are the same marriage under Latin and
    Croatian forms of both names — and both versions carry a daughter born
    <strong>19 April 1849</strong>, entered once as <em>Lucia</em> and once as <em>Luce</em>.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>Orešković and Sekula — an adoption, and why question 4d was hard</h2>
    <p class="lede">The 1835 Otočac baptism of <a href={u("/people/maria-catharina-oreskovic-oreskovic")}>Maria
    Catharina</a> enters her father as <strong><em>Marcus Oreskovich, adoptatus Sekula</em></strong>.
    The tree explains it: <strong>Marcus Orešković, born 18 April 1809, is the son of Vitus
    Sekula</strong>. He was born a Sekula and carries Orešković.</p>
    <p><strong>Then he married a Sekula</strong> — Lucia, born 14 April 1815, daughter of a
    <em>different</em> Sekula household — and <strong>two of their sons carry both surnames at
    once</strong>: <span class="mono">Franciscus Orešković Oreskovic Sekula</span> (1844) and
    <span class="mono">Petrus Orešković Oreskovic Sekula</span> (1846).</p>
    <p style="margin-top:1rem">Born one name, living under another, married back into the first,
    and passing both to his children. <strong>That is the same machine as
    <em>Antich Papa</em> becoming Papić</strong> — a surname changing hands over three generations
    — running in a different family at the same time. <a href={u("/name")}>The names →</a></p>

    <div class="note" style="margin-top:1.3rem"><strong>And it explains why question 4d was so
    hard.</strong> The Žubrinići married Oreškovići <strong>at least four times</strong>: Magdalena
    Orešković (b. 1796) married Nicolaus Žubrinić; Francisca (b. 1815) married Paulus Žubrinić;
    Maria Catharina (b. 1835) is given to Michael; Antonius Orešković married Helena Žubrinić.
    <strong>“A Maria Orešković married to a Michaël Žubrinić” was never going to identify anybody</strong>
    — the two families had been marrying each other for half a century.
    <a href={u("/open-questions")}>Question 4d →</a></div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>What these four have in common</h2>
    <p class="lede">None of them is on the direct line. All four change what the direct line
    <em>means</em>.</p>
    <ul class="plain">
      <li style="margin-bottom:.6rem"><strong>Repetition is the signal.</strong> Nine Šojat
        marriages, six Kostelac, four Orešković. A single marriage tells you two people met; nine
        tells you two villages were one community.</li>
      <li style="margin-bottom:.6rem"><strong>Names move between families.</strong> Šojat becomes a
        Blažević by-name; Sekula becomes an Orešković surname and then both; Papa becomes Papić.
        <strong>Three separate instances of one mechanism</strong>, and this archive has now caught
        all three.</li>
      <li><strong>And it is why identification keeps failing.</strong> When four Oreškovići marry
        four Žubrinići, a name and a surname identify nothing at all.
        <a href={u("/impossible")}>What cannot be true →</a></li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</Base>
